  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-12x+34)+{e}^{x}(2x-12) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22) \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-10x+22)+{e}^{x}(2x-10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=1,\\,$ $\\,x=4,\\,$ and $\\,x=7,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2033}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1-2)(1-6))=5e>0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(4) & ={e}^{4}((4-2)(4-6))=-4{e}^{4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(7) & ={e}^{7}((7-2)(7-6))=5{e}^{7}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2$ and $x=6$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2$ and $x=6,$ we conclude that $x=2$ and $x=6$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=2$ and $x=6$ are inflection points.",
